{"name":"Basilisk","hex":"#9ab38d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basilisk","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythical snake-like dragon, so venomous that even its gaze is deadly.","A type of dragon used in heraldry."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Suggesting a basilisk (snake-like dragon): baleful, spellbinding."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basilisk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Basilisk","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient European bestiaries and folklore, a basilisk is a legendary reptile reputed to be a serpent king, who causes any being to die when glanced at. According to the Naturalis Historia of Pliny the Elder, the basilisk of Cyrene is a small snake, \"being not more than twelve inches in length\", that is so venomous, it leaves a wide trail of deadly venom in its path, its gaze likewise just as lethal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Basilisk_aldrovandi.jpg/330px-Basilisk_aldrovandi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilisk","wikidata":"Q152519"},{"title":"Basilisk (manga)","blocks":[{"text":"Basilisk  is a Japanese manga series by Masaki Segawa, based on Futaro Yamada's 1958 novel The Kouga Ninja Scrolls. It was serialized in Kodansha's seinen manga magazine Young Magazine Uppers from February 2003 to July 2004. The story takes place in the year 1614. Two ninja clans, Iga of Tsubagakure and the Kouga of Manjidani, battle each other to determine which grandson of Tokugawa Ieyasu will become the next shogun. The deadly competition between ten elite ninja from each clan unleashes a centuries-old hatred that threatens to destroy all hope for peace between them."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/de/Basilisk_vol1_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilisk_(manga)","wikidata":"Q282464"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Sint_Michael_Zwolle_Stadszegel_1295.jpg/330px-Sint_Michael_Zwolle_Stadszegel_1295.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Sint_Michael_Zwolle_Stadszegel_1295.jpg/330px-Sint_Michael_Zwolle_Stadszegel_1295.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"City seal of Zwolle from 1295 with the Archangel Michael killing a basilisk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASint_Michael_Zwolle_Stadszegel_1295.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Marcus_Gheeraerts_I_-_Fable_of_the_basilisk_and_weasel.jpeg/500px-Marcus_Gheeraerts_I_-_Fable_of_the_basilisk_and_weasel.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Marcus_Gheeraerts_I_-_Fable_of_the_basilisk_and_weasel.jpeg/500px-Marcus_Gheeraerts_I_-_Fable_of_the_basilisk_and_weasel.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The basilisk and the weasel, by Marcus Gheeraerts the Elder. The cockatrice (pictured) became seen as synonymous with the basilisk when the \"basiliscus\" in Bartholomeus Anglicus's De proprietatibus rerum (ca 1260) was translated by John Trevisa as \"cockatrice\" (1397).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarcus_Gheeraerts_I_-_Fable_of_the_basilisk_and_weasel.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg/500px-Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg/1280px-Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg/500px-Muenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A putto kills a basilisk, symbolic of Swedish occupiers and Protestant heresy, on the Mariensäule, Munich, erected in 1638.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuenchen_Mariensaule_Putti_03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/IMG_3734_-_Milano_-_Stemma_visconteo-_sull%27Arcivescovado_-_Foto_di_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto_-_15-jan-2007.jpg/500px-IMG_3734_-_Milano_-_Stemma_visconteo-_sull%27Arcivescovado_-_Foto_di_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto_-_15-jan-2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/IMG_3734_-_Milano_-_Stemma_visconteo-_sull%27Arcivescovado_-_Foto_di_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto_-_15-jan-2007.jpg/500px-IMG_3734_-_Milano_-_Stemma_visconteo-_sull%27Arcivescovado_-_Foto_di_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto_-_15-jan-2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coat of arms, the biscione of the House of Visconti, on the Archbishops' palace in Piazza Duomo, Milan. The arms bear the initials IO.[HANNES] of Archbishop Giovanni Visconti (1342–1354).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIMG_3734_-_Milano_-_Stemma_visconteo-_sull'Arcivescovado_-_Foto_di_Giovanni_Dall'Orto_-_15-jan-2007.jpg"}],"sounds":{"basilisk":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/En-us-basilisk.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/8b/En-us-basilisk.ogg/En-us-basilisk.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Basilisk als Hölle","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Basilisk_als_H%C3%B6lle.jpg/500px-Basilisk_als_H%C3%B6lle.jpg","ratio":0.64377682